  "summary": "trimprompt ships heavily obfuscated modules combining child_process, HTTP POST and host identifiers",
  "details": "The trimprompt npm package ships a large set of source files (cache-manager.js, cache.js, ccr.js, dashboard.js, executor.js, file-watcher.js, filters/*.js, hooks/claude-hook.js, mcp.js, proxy-conv.js, proxy-resp.js, redactor.js, seed.js, simulate.js, sync.js, tracker.js) that are heavily obfuscated with hex-mangled identifiers (`_0x...`). Two of those modules (sync.js and tracker.js) additionally co-locate `require('child_process')`, HTTP `POST` calls, and host/identifier collection on a single minified line, and shims.js at line 67-126 wires `child_process` primitives including an `execSync('pwsh...')` invocation. The combination of pervasive whole-package obfuscation with child_process execution and outbound POST of host-identifier data is the operational shape of a credential/host exfiltration and command-execution payload rather than a legitimate developer tool. Files named `hooks/claude-hook.js`, `mcp.js`, `proxy-conv.js`, and `proxy-resp.js` indicate the package inserts itself into an AI coding-assistant tool chain, giving the obfuscated code a position to observe prompts, tokens, and shell activity on the developer's machine.",
